      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "The antiderivative of $\\sqrt[3]{x}$ equals{{field-1}} $+\\,\\text{\u00a0}C$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Since $\\sqrt[3]{x}={x}^{1/3}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/3+1}}{(\\frac{1}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4/3}}{(\\frac{4}{3})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4/3})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}{x}^{4/3-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/3}\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/id": "7ca5a02b-2795-5ad8-b27b-aca10004dde5",
