      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "The antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{x}$ equals\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $+\\,\\text{\u00a0}C$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Since $\\sqrt[5]{x}={x}^{1/5}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/5+1}}{(\\frac{1}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/5}}{(\\frac{6}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{6}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{6/5})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot \\frac{6}{5}{x}^{6/5-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/5}\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$",
